[Chapter "Key ideas: Why the Caro-Kann"]
[Type "ideas"]

{Why play the Caro-Kann? It is solid and simple. Black builds a sound pawn center that White's pieces can't easily attack, and the same few ideas work against almost everything White tries.

Compare 1...e5. There White can choose the Italian, the Scotch, the Vienna, the King's Gambit and many more, and several of them aim straight at f7. After 1...c6 and ...d5 there is no bishop attack on f7, because the pawn on d5 controls c4.

Compare the French, 1...e6. It also prepares ...d5, but the pawn on e6 shuts in the c8 bishop. In the Caro-Kann that bishop comes out first, to f5 or g4, and only then does Black play ...e6.

The price: Black has a little less space, and the c-pawn takes c6 away from the b8 knight. That is why White's main tries all aim to cramp Black's pieces.

The opening is named after Horatio Caro, an English player, and Marcus Kann, an Austrian, who published their analysis in 1886. Kann had already used it to beat Jacques Mieses in 24 moves at Hamburg in 1885. Capablanca played it, and the main line 4...Bf5 is also called the Capablanca Variation. Botvinnik relied on it in his world championship matches against Tal in 1960 and 1961, and Karpov played 4...Nd7 so often that the line usually carries his name. Ding Liren and Alireza Firouzja play it today.

Step through the moves to see the idea.}
1. e4 c6 {[%cal Gc6d5] Prepares ...d5. If White ever takes on d5, Black recaptures with the c-pawn and keeps a pawn in the center.}
2. d4 d5 {[%cal Rd5e4,Gc8f5,Gc8g4] Black attacks e4. White has to decide what to do with the pawn, and each choice leads to its own structure:

Take on d5: the Exchange (3.exd5 cxd5 4.Bd3) or the Panov (4.c4).

Push to e5: the Advance. We answer 3...c5.

Protect it with 3.Nc3 or 3.Nd2: the Classical. We take on e4 and play 4...Bf5.

Protect it with 3.f3: the Fantasy. We take on e4 and strike back with ...e5.

The green arrows show where the c8 bishop wants to go, before ...e6 shuts it in.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Plans for both sides"]
[Type "ideas"]
[FEN "r4rk1/ppqnbpp1/2p1pn1p/7P/2PP4/3Q1NN1/PP1B1PP1/1K1R3R w - - 5 17"]

{[%cal Gc6c5,Ge6e5,Rg2g4,Rg4g5] [%csl Yh5,Yh6] The golden rule of the Caro-Kann: bring the c8 bishop out before ...e6. Then ...e6 completes a solid wall of pawns, and the rest of the pieces develop behind it.

This position comes from the main line of the Classical, 3.Nc3 dxe4 4.Nxe4 Bf5. Black's bishop went f5-g6-h7 and was traded on d3. Stockfish rates the position as equal.

Classical (3.Nc3 dxe4 4.Nxe4 Bf5): White gains time chasing the bishop with Ng3 and h4-h5, then usually castles queenside. Black answers h4 with ...h6, trades the bishop on d3, and uses ...Qa5+ and ...Bb4 to provoke c3 and c4 before the bishop drops back to e7. Black's freeing breaks are ...c5 and ...e5 (green arrows). With White's pawn on h5, the pawn on h6 is a hook: White can open the g-file with g4-g5 (red arrows). So Black castles kingside only when ready to counterattack with ...c5.

Advance (3.e5 c5): White has space; Black attacks d4, the base of White's chain. Develop with ...Nc6, pin with ...Bg4 once the knight is on f3 and trade it off, play ...e6 only after the bishop is out, then bring the other knight to f5 via e7 and add ...Qb6.

Exchange (3.exd5 cxd5 4.Bd3): White aims the pieces at Black's kingside. Black develops with ...Nc6, ...Nf6, ...Bg4 or ...Bf5, ...e6 and ...Bd6, then plays on the queenside with ...b5-b4.

Panov (3.exd5 cxd5 4.c4): White accepts an isolated d-pawn for quick, active development. Black develops fast, blocks the d-pawn with a knight on d5, and trades pieces. Endgames favor Black.

Fantasy (3.f3): White wants to keep two pawns in the center. Black takes on e4, strikes back with ...e5, and uses the dark diagonals that f3 weakened: ...Qh4+ and ...Bc5.

Two Knights (2.Nc3 d5 3.Nf3): White delays d4 to set traps. Pin with ...Bg4, take on f3 when h3 asks the question, then play ...e6 and ...Nf6. Black is solid and comfortable.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Pawn structures"]
[Type "ideas"]
[FEN "r2qkb1r/pp2nppp/2n1p3/3pP3/3P2b1/5N2/PP2BPPP/RNBQ1RK1 w kq - 2 9"]

{[%cal Gc6d4,Gg4f3,Ge7f5,Gd8b6] [%csl Yd4] Five structures cover most Caro-Kann middlegames. Learn what each side wants in each one.

The Advance chain (this position, from 3.e5 c5 4.c3 Nc6 5.Nf3 cxd4 6.cxd4 Bg4 7.Be2 e6 8.O-O Nge7). White's pawns on d4 and e5 form a chain, and d4 is its base. No White pawn can defend d4 any more, so Black aims everything at it: the knight on c6, ...Bxf3 to remove a defender, ...Nf5 and ...Qb6. The other break, ...f6, attacks the tip on e5. It also loosens Black's king, so it works best once the light-squared bishops are off.

The Classical structure (3.Nc3 dxe4 4.Nxe4). There are no e-pawns. White's pawn on d4 gives more space; Black's pawns on c6 and e6 make a solid wall. Black frees the game with ...c5 or ...e5. If White's h-pawn reaches h5, it cramps Black's kingside, so Black often castles queenside or delays castling.

The Carlsbad (the Exchange, 3.exd5 cxd5 4.Bd3). White has three pawns against two on the queenside (a, b, c against a, b). Black uses the two to attack the three: ...b5 and ...b4, the minority attack, to leave White with a weak pawn on c3. White's plan is a kingside attack with Bd3, Bf4, the queen, a knight on e5 and a rook lift.

The isolated d-pawn (the Panov, 4.c4, after Black takes on c4). White's d4 pawn has no neighbors. It gives White active pieces and the outposts e5 and c5, but it is a weakness in the endgame. Black blockades it with a knight on d5 and trades pieces. If White pushes c4-c5 instead, White gets three pawns against two on the queenside, and Black must strike back at once, with ...b6 against c5 or ...e5 against d4.

The Fantasy center (3.f3 dxe4 4.fxe4 e5). White keeps pawns on e4 and d4 and the half-open f-file. Black's ...e5 challenges them at once, and the diagonals to White's king (e1-h4 and g1-a7) are weak.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Tricks and traps"]
[Type "ideas"]
[FEN "rn1qkbnr/pp2pppp/2p3b1/8/3P3P/6N1/PPP2PP1/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 0 6"]

{[%cal Rh4h5,Gh7h6] [%csl Rg6] Six patterns to know.

Give the bishop a square. In this position from the Classical, 6.h4 threatens h5, which drives the bishop from g6, and the knight on g3 then takes it on f5. Only 6...h6 keeps the balance. After 6...e6? 7.h5 the bishop has to go to f5, where the knight takes it. 8.Nxf5 exf5 leaves Black with doubled f-pawns, a weak pawn on f5, and no bishop pair against White's two bishops (Stockfish +2.8). The same lesson applies against the Advance: after 3...Bf5 4.h4, the natural 4...e6?? loses the bishop to 5.g4 and h5. That is one reason we answer the Advance with 3...c5.

The smothered mate. After 3.Nc3 dxe4 4.Nxe4 Nd7 5.Qe2, the natural 5...Ngf6?? walks into 6.Nd6 mate: the queen on e2 pins the e7 pawn, so it can't take the knight. Play 5...Ndf6 instead.

Sacrifices on f7 and e6. When White has a bishop on c4, a knight on g5 and the queen on e2, don't kick the knight with ...h6 too early. In the Karpov line 5.Bc4 Ngf6 6.Ng5 e6 7.Qe2, the move 7...h6?? loses to 8.Nxf7!, and Stockfish rates it +9.4. 7...Nb6 is correct.

The Fantasy's weak diagonal. After 3.f3 dxe4 4.fxe4 e5, White must not take on e5. 5.dxe5? Qh4+ wins the e4 pawn with check (Stockfish −1.1), and 6.g3? Qxe4+ also hits the rook on h1.

The f3 gambit. After 3.Nc3 dxe4 4.f3 exf3 5.Nxf3 Bg4 6.Bc4, Black must play 6...e6 before ...Nf6. After 6...Nf6?, 7.Bxf7+ Kxf7 8.Ne5+ forks the king and the bishop on g4 (Stockfish +2.2). And 7.Ne5 Bxd1?? 8.Bxf7 is mate.

Take the knight when asked. In the Two Knights, after 3...Bg4 4.h3, play 4...Bxf3. Keeping the pin with 4...Bh5?! lets White gain space with 5.exd5 cxd5 6.g4 and 7.Ne5.} *

[Chapter "Main line: 4...Bf5"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Classical Variation, Main Line"]
[ECO "B18"]

{The Classical Variation, the main line of the Caro-Kann for more than a century. Black develops the bishop at once and lets White chase it. In return Black gets a rock-solid position with no weaknesses. White can deviate at many points after move 5; the notes give the plan against each, and the ideas hardly change.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5
3. Nc3 {Defends e4 with the knight. 3.Nd2 does the same job, and after 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 the position is identical.}
3... dxe4 {Takes the pawn. The c8 bishop has no good square yet, and 3...Nf6 would be chased away by e5.}
4. Nxe4 Bf5 {[%cal Gf5e4] The point of the Caro-Kann: the bishop comes out before ...e6, and it gains time by hitting the knight.}
5. Ng3 {The knight steps back and attacks the bishop in turn.

Other fifth moves. 5.Bd3 offers a bishop trade, but the bishop blocks the queen's guard of d4: take the pawn with 5...Qxd4 and bring the queen home to d8. Black is a pawn up, and White's lead in development only makes it about equal. 5.Nc5 hits b7: challenge the knight with 5...Nd7, and trade bishops if White plays Bd3.} Bg6 {The bishop keeps its diagonal.}
6. h4 {[%cal Gh4h5] Threatens h5, which would drive the bishop off g6.

White can also delay h4. Against 6.Nf3, play ...Nd7 and ...e6; if White offers the trade with Bd3, you can let White take on g6, because after ...hxg6 your rook gets the half-open h-file. Against 6.N1e2 or 6.Nh3, both heading for f4 to chase the bishop, develop with ...Nf6 and give the bishop h7 with ...h6; after 6.Nh3 Nf6 7.Nf4, the central strike 7...e5! also works. Against 6.Bc4, close the diagonal with ...e6. Against 6.f4, which threatens f5, play ...e6 and later stop the h-pawn with ...h5. The ideas never change: ...Nd7, ...e6, ...h6 against h4, and a calm bishop trade.} h6! {Makes room for the bishop on h7. The only move that keeps the balance.}
(6... e6? {Natural, but too slow.} 7. h5 Bf5 8. Nxf5 exf5 {Black's f-pawns are doubled and the one on f5 is weak, while White has the two bishops: Stockfish +2.8.})
7. Nf3 {Develops and eyes e5. 7.h5 Bh7 8.Nf3 Nd7 reaches the main line by a different move order.}
7... Nd7 {Guards e5, so White's knight can't jump there, and prepares ...Ngf6. Stockfish prefers it slightly to 7...Nf6, which allows 8.Ne5.}
8. h5 {[%csl Yh5,Yh6] Gains space and fixes Black's pawn on h6.

If White offers the trade at once with 8.Bd3, take first: 8...Bxd3 9.Qxd3 e6, with the usual setup. Don't play 8...Bh7?, because 9.Bxh7 Rxh7 misplaces the rook, and never 8...e6??, because 9.Bxg6 fxg6 wrecks your pawns (Stockfish +3.5).}
8... Bh7 9. Bd3 {Offers to trade the bishop that Black has already moved four times.} Bxd3 10. Qxd3 e6 {[%cal Gc6d5,Ge6d5] The light-squared bishops are off, so ...e6 locks nothing in. Black's pawns on c6 and e6 form a solid wall.}
11. Bd2 {The classical main line. White prepares to castle queenside.

The modern 11.Bf4 is met by 11...Qa5+. After 12.Bd2 Bb4 13.c3 Be7 14.c4 Qc7 15.O-O-O Ngf6 16.Kb1 O-O, Black has provoked c3 and c4 at no cost, and the position is equal. Against 12.Qd2 or 12.c3, the same ...Bb4 and ...Ngf6 development works.}
11... Ngf6 12. O-O-O Be7 {Black keeps the king flexible. Castling on either side is still possible.}
13. Ne4 {Offers a knight trade that brings White's queen to the center. If 13.Kb1, castle and prepare ...c5.}
13... Nxe4 14. Qxe4 Nf6 {The other knight comes to f6 with a gain of time on the queen.}
15. Qe2 Qd5 {[%cal Gd5a2] The queen centralizes, eyes a2 next to White's king, and offers a trade on e4.}
16. c4 {Kicks the queen.} Qe4 {Black offers the trade anyway. Without queens, White's attacking chances are gone.}
17. Qxe4 Nxe4 {A level endgame. Black's pawns have no weaknesses, while White's pawn on h5 is far from home and can become a target.} *

[Chapter "Advance: 3...c5"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Advance Variation, Botvinnik-Carls Defense"]
[ECO "B12"]

{Against the Advance we hit the base of White's pawn chain at once. Black spends two moves on the c-pawn, but White's e5 push didn't develop a piece either. Botvinnik used 3...c5 in his 1961 match against Tal. The critical reply is 4.dxc5. The next chapter covers 4.c3; White's other fourth moves are under Advanced variations.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 {The Advance.} c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits d4, the base of White's chain, before developing the bishop.}
4. dxc5 {The critical reply: White gives up the d-pawn and hopes to keep the extra pawn on c5.} e6 {Opens the f8 bishop's diagonal, so ...Bxc5 will regain the pawn. Stockfish prefers it to 4...Nc6, which allows 5.f4.}
5. Nf3 {Other fifth moves. Against 5.Bd3, play ...Nd7, which hits c5 and e5, and take back on c5 with the knight. Against 5.Be3, which guards c5, bring a knight to f5 via e7 to hit the bishop. Against 5.Qg4, kick the queen with ...h5!, then win back c5 with ...Nc6 and ...Bxc5. In every case Black regains the pawn.}
5... Bxc5 {Pawn regained. Black's c8 bishop is behind the e6 pawn, but White's d-pawn is gone, so Black has a free and easy game.}
6. Bd3 Nc6 7. O-O Nge7 {[%cal Ge7g6,Gg6e5] The knight heads for g6 to hit e5.}
8. a3 a6 {Keeps White's pieces off b5.} 9. Qe2 O-O {Black has castled. Next ...Ng6 and ...f6 attack e5. Equal.} *

[Chapter "Advance: 4.c3"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Advance Variation, Botvinnik-Carls Defense"]
[ECO "B12"]

{When White holds the chain with c3, Black's plan works smoothly: ...Nc6, take on d4, pin with ...Bg4, and bring a knight to f5. Every Black piece hits d4.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5
4. c3 {Supports the chain, French style.}
4... Nc6 {Adds pressure on d4.}
5. Nf3 cxd4! {Take first. 5...Bg4 at once allows 6.dxc5 (Stockfish +0.5).}
6. cxd4 {Now no White pawn can ever defend d4.} Bg4 {[%cal Gg4f3] Pins the knight that guards d4.}
7. Be2 e6 {Only now, with the bishop outside the chain, does the e-pawn move.}
8. O-O Nge7 {Heads for f5.} 9. Nbd2 Nf5 {[%cal Gf5d4,Gc6d4] Two knights hit d4. Next ...Qb6 and ...Be7. Stockfish rates White slightly better, but Black's plan is easy to follow.} *

[Chapter "Exchange: 4.Bd3 and 4.Nf3"]
[Diagram "4.Bd3"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Exchange Variation, Rubinstein Variation"]
[ECO "B13"]

{White trades on d5 and develops toward Black's king. The c-pawn recaptures toward the center, and c6 is free for the knight. This is the Carlsbad structure: Black plays on the queenside with the minority attack, ...b5 and ...b4.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 {Recapture with the pawn and keep a pawn in the center.}
4. Bd3 {Develops toward the kingside and keeps Black's bishop off f5. 4.Bf4 usually comes back to this line: see Advanced variations.}
(4. Nf3 Nc6 {The same position as 2.Nf3 d5 3.exd5 cxd5 4.d4 Nc6, which comes up more often: see the chapter "2.Nf3: 3.exd5".})
4... Nc6 {Attacks d4 and prepares ...Bg4.}
5. c3 {If 5.Nf3, pin with ...Bg4 at once, then play ...e6 and ...Bd6 to trade the dark-squared bishops.}
5... Nf6
6. Bf4 {White's ideal setup: the bishops on d3 and f4 rake Black's kingside. If 6.Nf3, pin with ...Bg4; after h3 Bh5 and Bf4, offer the trade of bishops with ...Bd6.}
6... Bg4 {Develops with a threat to the queen, so White can't play Nf3 without allowing a pin.}
7. Qb3 {Attacks b7.} Qd7 {Guards b7 along the seventh rank.}
8. Nd2 e6 9. Ngf3 Bxf3 {Gives up the bishop to finish development quickly.} 10. Nxf3 Bd6 {Offers to trade White's best minor piece.}
11. Bxd6 Qxd6 12. O-O O-O 13. Rfe1 Rab8 {[%cal Gb7b5,Gb5b4] The minority attack: next ...b5 and ...b4, aiming to leave White with a weak pawn on c3 or d4. Equal.} *

[Chapter "Panov: 4.c4"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Panov Attack"]
[ECO "B13"]

{White hits d5 again and often ends up with an isolated d-pawn. We develop solidly with ...Nf6, ...e6 and ...Be7, then castle. Against the isolated pawn, blockade d5 and trade pieces.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. c4 Nf6 {Defends d5 and develops.}
5. Nc3 {If 5.c5, White grabs space but releases the pressure on d5: undermine the chain at once with ...b6, and strike with ...e5 if White's pawns get loose. 5.Nf3 e6 6.Nc3 Be7 transposes.}
5... e6 {Supports d5 with a pawn. The c8 bishop is shut in for now, but against the Panov, a solid d5 matters more.}
6. Nf3 {If 6.Bg5, simply ...Be7 and castle: the pin is harmless.}
6... Be7
7. cxd5 {White releases the tension. After the recapture, White's d-pawn is isolated.

If 7.c5, castle and hit the chain with ...b6 before White's queenside majority can roll. If 7.Bd3, take with ...dxc4 and, after Bxc4, castle and blockade the isolated d-pawn with a knight on d5.}
7... Nxd5 8. Bd3 Nc6 9. O-O O-O 10. Re1 Bf6 {[%cal Gf6d4,Gc6d4] Black piles up on the isolated pawn while the knight on d5 blockades it. Equal.} *

[Chapter "2.Nf3: 3.exd5"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense"]
[ECO "B10"]

{On Lichess, 2.Nf3 is White's second most common move after 2.d4. Black still plays ...d5. White usually trades with 3.exd5 and then plays d4, reaching the Exchange Variation with the knight on f3. Black's setup is the one from the Exchange chapter: ...Nc6, ...Bg4 to pin the knight, ...e6 and ...Bd6.}
1. e4 c6 2. Nf3 {Develops and keeps the choice between d4 and d3.} d5 {Black takes a share of the center, as always.}
3. exd5 {White's most common choice by far. 3.e5 is in the next chapter; 3.Nc3 leads to the Two Knights (under Advanced variations). Against 3.d3, trade queens with 3...dxe4 4.dxe4 Qxd1+ 5.Kxd1: White can no longer castle, and the endgame is equal.}
3... cxd5 {Recapture with the pawn and keep a pawn in the center.}
4. d4 {White plays d4 in four games out of five. The position is the same as after 2.d4 d5 3.exd5 cxd5 4.Nf3.

Other fourth moves. Against 4.Bb5+, block with 4...Bd7 and offer the bishop trade, or play 4...Nc6. Against 4.Nc3, develop with ...Nf6 or ...Nc6 and pin with ...Bg4. Against 4.c4, play 4...Nf6; after cxd5 Nxd5 you have a Panov-type position, so blockade d5 and develop.}
4... Nc6 {Hits d4 and prepares ...Bg4, as in the Exchange chapter.}
5. Bb5 {Pins the knight. White's most common choice.

Other fifth moves. Against 5.Nc3, 5.c3 or 5.Bd3, pin with 5...Bg4, then play ...e6, ...Nf6 and ...Bd6. Against 5.c4, play 5...Nf6; after 6.cxd5 Nxd5, blockade d5 as in the Panov.}
5... Bg4 {[%cal Gg4f3] Pins the knight that guards d4.}
6. O-O {If 6.h3, take at once: 6...Bxf3 7.Qxf3 e6, then ...Bd6 and ...Nge7. Equal. If 6.Bxc6+ bxc6, the c-pawns guard d5 and Black gets the half-open b-file.}
6... e6 {Opens the f8 bishop's diagonal and supports d5.}
7. h3 Bh5 {Keeps the pin. The bishop can still take on f3 later.}
8. Bf4 Bd6 {Offers to trade White's active bishop, as in the Exchange chapter.}
9. Bxd6 Qxd6 10. c3 Ne7 {The knight supports c6 and can go to f5 or g6.}
11. Nbd2 a6 {Asks the bishop to decide.} 12. Bd3 O-O {Black has castled with a solid center. Equal.} *

[Chapter "2.Nf3: 3.e5"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense"]
[ECO "B10"]

{After 2.Nf3 d5, White can also gain space with 3.e5. Bring the bishop to g4 before ...e6, then strike with ...c5: the bishop stays outside the pawn chain, as in the Two Knights.}
1. e4 c6 2. Nf3 d5
3. e5 {Gains space, but without d4 the center is less firm.} Bg4 {Black's most popular reply: the bishop comes out before ...e6.}
4. d4 {White's usual move. Against 4.Be2, play ...e6 and ...c5 the same way. Against 4.h3, take: 4...Bxf3 5.Qxf3 e6, then ...c5. Equal.}
4... e6 {Now the pawn chain doesn't shut in the bishop.}
5. Be2 {Against 5.h3, take: 5...Bxf3 6.Qxf3 c5, and Black hits d4 at once. Equal.}
5... c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits the base of the chain, as in the Advance chapters.}
6. O-O {Against 6.c3, develop with 6...Nc6 and ...Nge7 before taking on d4. Against 6.dxc5, take back with 6...Bxc5. Both are equal.}
6... Nc6 7. c3 cxd4 8. Nxd4 Bxe2 {Trades the light-squared bishops.} 9. Qxe2 Nge7 {Next ...Nxd4 or ...Ng6, and ...Be7. Equal.} *

[Chapter "Other second moves: 2.Bc4 and 2.c4"]
[Diagram "2.Bc4"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Hillbilly Attack"]
[ECO "B10"]

{Against White's rarer second moves, Black still plays ...d5 and gets an easy game. 2.d3 is under Advanced variations.}
1. e4 c6
2. Bc4 {The Hillbilly Attack. The bishop runs into ...d5.}
(2. c4 {The Accelerated Panov.} d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. cxd5 Nf6 {The knight will take back on d5.} 5. Nc3 Nxd5 6. Nf3 Nc6 {Equal. Black's development is easy.})
2... d5 3. exd5 {3.Bb3, the Schaeffer Gambit, is under Advanced variations.} cxd5 4. Bb5+ Bd7 {Offers the bishop trade. Black is at least equal.} *

[Chapter "Karpov: 4...Nd7"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Karpov Variation"]
[ECO "B17"]

{Karpov's favorite, and a second weapon next to 4...Bf5. Black prepares ...Ngf6 so that if White trades on f6, a knight recaptures and Black keeps a healthy pawn structure. The cost: the c8 bishop waits a little longer. Before you play it, learn the smothered-mate trap in the chapter "Trap: 5.Qe2 and the smothered mate".}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7
5. Nf3 {The most natural move.

Other fifth moves. Against 5.Ng5 (the modern main line) or 5.Bd3, develop with ...Ngf6, ...e6 and ...Bd6, and kick the knight with ...h6 only once e6 and f7 are covered; then ...Qc7 and the ...c5 break. Against 5.Bc4 Ngf6 6.Ng5 e6 7.Qe2, play 7...Nb6!, which drives the bishop off the a2-g8 diagonal. Never 7...h6?? there, because 8.Nxf7! tears Black's position open (Stockfish +9.4).} Ngf6
6. Nxf6+ {White trades, but Black recaptures with a knight, so the pawn structure stays intact. That is the point of 4...Nd7. If 6.Ng3 instead, play ...e6 and the ...c5 break.}
6... Nxf6
7. Bc4 {Aims at f7 and prepares Ne5. Against 7.c3, develop the bishop first with ...Bg4. Against 7.Ne5, guard f7 with ...Be6 and fianchetto the other bishop.}
7... Bf5 {The bishop gets out first.} 8. O-O e6 9. c3 Bd6 {Black is fully developed and safe. Equal.} *

[Chapter "Classical: 3.Nd2"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Classical Variation"]
[ECO "B18"]

{3.Nd2 defends e4 just as 3.Nc3 does, and after 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 the position is the same. Play the Classical with 4...Bf5.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nd2 {Does the same job as 3.Nc3.} dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 {Back in the main line: see "Main line: 4...Bf5".} *

[Chapter "Advance: other fourth moves"]
[Diagram "4.Nf3"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Advance Variation, Botvinnik-Carls Defense"]
[ECO "B12"]

{White's rarer fourth moves against 3...c5. Against 4.Nf3, take on d4 at once. Against the check, offer the bishop trade: it removes Black's problem bishop. Against 4.c4, develop and take on d4.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5
4. Nf3 {Develops, but leaves d4 without pawn support.}
(4. Bb5+ {Checks before Black has developed.} Bd7 {Offers the bishop trade.} 5. Bxd7+ Qxd7 6. c3 cxd4 7. cxd4 Nc6 {d4 is a target, and Black no longer has a bad bishop to worry about. Equal.})
(4. c4 {Hits d5 from the side.} Nc6 5. Nf3 cxd4 6. Nxd4 e6 7. Nc3 Bc5 {Develops with a gain of time on the knight. Equal.})
4... cxd4 {Take at once: White can't recapture with a pawn.}
5. Qxd4 Nc6 {Gains time on the queen.} 6. Qf4 e6 7. Bd3 Nb4! {[%cal Gb4d3] Goes after White's good bishop.} 8. Nc3 Nxd3+ 9. cxd3 Ne7 {Black has the bishop pair. Next ...Bd7 and ...Nc6 or ...Nf5. Stockfish rates White slightly better.} *

[Chapter "Exchange: 4.Bf4"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Exchange Variation, Rubinstein Variation"]
[ECO "B13"]

{White develops the dark-squared bishop first. Black's setup is the same as against 4.Bd3, and the moves usually come back to that line.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. Bf4 Nc6 5. c3 Nf6 6. Bd3 Bg4 {The main line of the Exchange by a different move order.} *

[Chapter "Two Knights: 3...Bg4"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Two Knights Attack, Mindeno Variation, Exchange Line"]
[ECO "B11"]

{White develops both knights before deciding on d4, and the lines after 3...dxe4 are full of traps. We pin with 3...Bg4 and give up the bishop when h3 asks. Then Black's pawns go to light squares (c6, d5, e6), where White's remaining light-squared bishop can't attack them. The same position often comes from 2.Nf3 d5 3.Nc3.}
1. e4 c6 2. Nc3
(2. Nf3 d5 3. Nc3 Bg4 {The same position by another move order.})
2... d5 3. Nf3 Bg4 {Pins the knight.}
4. h3
(4. Be2 Nf6 {Develops and hits e4.} 5. e5 Nfd7 6. d4 e6 {A French structure, but Black's light-squared bishop is already outside the chain. Equal.})
(4. d4 e6 {Supports d5.} 5. Be2 Nd7 {Prepares ...Ngf6. Solid, with the bishop outside the pawn chain.})
4... Bxf3 {Take when asked.}
(4... Bh5?! {Keeps the pin but loses time.} 5. exd5 cxd5 6. g4 Bg6 7. Ne5 {White gains space and time against the bishop: Stockfish +0.7.})
5. Qxf3 e6 {[%csl Yc6,Yd5,Ye6] The pawn wall on light squares.}
6. d4
(6. d3 Nf6 7. Be2 Nbd7 8. O-O Bc5 {Black develops freely. Equal.})
(6. Be2 Nf6 7. O-O Be7 {Calm development. Equal.})
6... Nf6 7. Bd3 dxe4 8. Nxe4 Qxd4 {Takes the pawn. Black must finish developing quickly, with ...Nbd7, ...Be7 and ...O-O.}
9. O-O Nbd7 {Stockfish rates it equal: White has development for the pawn.} *

[Chapter "Fantasy: 3...dxe4"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Maróczy Variation"]
[ECO "B12"]

{f3 keeps a pawn on e4: after ...dxe4 fxe4, White has two center pawns and the half-open f-file. But f3 weakens the diagonals to White's king, e1-h4 and g1-a7. We take on e4 and hit back with ...e5 at once.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. f3 dxe4 4. fxe4 e5 {[%cal Ge5d4,Rd8h4] Strikes at d4 before White is developed. Taking with 5.dxe5? runs into ...Qh4+ (see the trap chapter).}
5. Nf3 {Attacks e5 and covers h4.}
(5. Nc3? exd4 {Hits the knight.} 6. Nce2 Nf6 {Black is a clean pawn up, and e4 is weak too: Stockfish −1.9.})
(5. Bc4 Qxd4 {With no knight on f3, the d4 pawn is simply loose.} 6. Qxd4 exd4 {Black is a pawn up in an early endgame.})
(5. Be3 Qh4+ {The weakened diagonal.} 6. Bf2 Qxe4+ 7. Qe2 Qxe2+ 8. Nxe2 exd4 {A pawn up in the endgame: Stockfish −1.1.})
5... Bg4 {[%cal Gg4f3] Pins the knight that guards d4.}
6. Bc4 {Aims at f7.}
(6. c3 Nd7 7. Be2 Ngf6 8. O-O Bd6 {The same setup. The e5 pawn holds firm. Equal.})
6... Nd7 {Supports e5 and prepares ...Ngf6.}
7. O-O Ngf6 {Not 7...Bxf3? 8.Qxf3, when f7 comes under fire.} 8. c3 Bd6 {Black has developed everything and keeps a strong pawn on e5. Equal.} *

[Chapter "Other second moves: 2.d3"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Breyer Variation"]
[ECO "B10"]

{2.d3 is the King's Indian Attack setup: d3, Nd2, Ngf3, g3, Bg2 and O-O, whatever Black does. With White's pawn on d3, Black can take a big center with ...d5 and ...e5.}
1. e4 c6 2. d3 {The King's Indian Attack setup.} d5 3. Nd2 e5 {With White's pawn on d3, Black can take a big center with ...e5.}
4. Ngf3 Bd6 5. g3 Nf6 6. Bg2 O-O 7. O-O Re8 {Black has a fine center and well-placed pieces. Equal.} *

[Chapter "Hillbilly: 3.Bb3"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Hillbilly Attack, Schaeffer Gambit"]
[ECO "B10"]

{After 2.Bc4 d5, White can keep the bishop on the diagonal with 3.Bb3 and gambit the e-pawn. Take it, and meet the queen sortie by blocking with ...g6.}
1. e4 c6 2. Bc4 d5 3. Bb3 dxe4 4. Qh5 {The Schaeffer Gambit: queen and bishop hit f7.} g6! {Blocks the queen, and Black stays a pawn up: Stockfish −0.7. Don't play 4...Nf6??, which allows 5.Qxf7+: after 5...Kd7 Black's king is stuck in the open (Stockfish +3.2).} *

[Chapter "Trap: 5.Qe2 and the smothered mate"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Karpov Variation"]
[ECO "B17"]

{If you play the Karpov line, 4...Nd7, know this trap. With White's queen on e2, Black's e7 pawn is pinned to the king, so a knight that lands on d6 can't be taken.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7
5. Qe2 {[%cal Ge4d6,Ge2e7] Sets the trap.} Ndf6! {Moves the d7 knight, so the king gets d7 as an escape square, and challenges the knight on e4.}
(5... Ngf6?? {The natural move, and it loses at once.} 6. Nd6# {Smothered mate. The e7 pawn is pinned, and Black's own pieces box in the king.})
6. Nf3 {If 6.Nxf6+, recapture with the knight and develop normally.}
6... Nxe4 7. Qxe4 Nf6 {Black has traded off the e4 knight and gains time on the queen. Equal.} *

[Chapter "Trap: 5.dxe5 and ...Qh4+"]
[Diagram "5.dxe5"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Maróczy Variation"]
[ECO "B12"]

{In the Fantasy, after 3...dxe4 4.fxe4 e5, the pawn on e5 looks free. It isn't: f3 has opened the e1-h4 diagonal to White's king.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. f3 dxe4 4. fxe4 e5
5. dxe5? {Grabs the pawn.} Qh4+! {[%cal Gh4e1,Gh4e4] Check, and the queen eyes the loose pawn on e4.}
6. Kd2 {White's best try. 6.g3? loses the rook on h1 to 6...Qxe4+, a fork of king and rook (Stockfish −7.2). 6.Ke2? Qxe4+ 7.Be3 Bc5 leaves the king exposed to all of Black's pieces (Stockfish −2.9).}
6... Qf4+ 7. Ke1 Qxe4+ {Wins the pawn back with check.} 8. Qe2 Bf5 {Black has regained the pawn, White's king can no longer castle, and the pawn on e5 is weak. Stockfish −1.2.} *

[Chapter "Trap: the 3...e5 gambit"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Maróczy Variation"]
[ECO "B12"]

{A surprise weapon against the Fantasy, popular in online games. Black gives up a pawn to open the dark diagonals that f3 weakened, and sets a mating trap that catches many club players. It is risky: against the best defense, 5.Nc3! and 6.Na4, White is slightly better (Stockfish +0.5). Use it as a surprise, not as your main weapon.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. f3 e5 {[%cal Gd8h4,Gf8c5] Opens lines toward e1 and g1.}
4. dxe5 {The most common reply: White takes the pawn.}
(4. exd5 exd4 5. Qxd4 cxd5 {Equal. Black's development is easy.})
(4. Nc3 dxe4 5. dxe5 Qxd1+ 6. Nxd1 Nd7 {Black wins back the pawn on e5. Equal.})
4... Qb6 {[%cal Gb6g1] Eyes g1 and b2, and makes room for ...Bc5.}
5. exd5 {Grabs a second pawn. It looks greedy, and it is.

The best defense is 5.Nc3! Bc5 6.Na4!, hitting the queen and the bishop at once. After 6...Bf2+ 7.Ke2 Qd4 8.Qxd4 Bxd4, Black has lost the gamble, and White is slightly better.}
5... Bc5 {[%cal Gc5g1,Gb6g1] Two pieces hit g1, and the knight there has no good square.}
6. Ne2?? {The most common reply, and it loses at once. The only good defense is 6.Nh3!: after 6...Bxh3 7.gxh3 Bf2+ 8.Ke2 cxd5, Black has won back material, White's king is stuck in the center, and the game is equal.}
6... Bf2+ 7. Kd2 Qe3# {Mate. The king has no squares.} *

[Chapter "Trap: the 4.f3 gambit"]
[Diagram "4.f3"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Rasa-Studier Gambit"]
[ECO "B15"]

{Some White players gamble with 3.Nc3 dxe4 4.f3, hoping for quick development and an attack on the f-file. Take the pawn and develop calmly: Black is better. One trap to know: once your bishop is on g4 and White's on c4, play ...e6 before ...Nf6.}
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. f3 exf3 {Accept: the extra pawn is safe.}
5. Nxf3 Bg4 {Pins the knight.}
6. Bc4 {[%cal Gc4f7] Aims at f7. If 6.h3 instead, take on f3 and grab a second pawn with ...Qxd4 (Stockfish −0.4). If 6.Be3, ...Nf6 is fine, because there is no bishop on c4.}
6... e6! {Shuts the bishop off from f7.}
(6... Nf6? 7. Bxf7+! Kxf7 8. Ne5+ {Forks the king and the bishop on g4: Stockfish +2.2. And if 7.Ne5 instead, then 7...Bxd1?? 8.Bxf7 is mate.})
7. O-O Be7 8. h3 Bh5 {Black is a pawn up with a solid position: Stockfish −0.8.} *
